✅ 1. Declaration & Initialization
String str = "Deepak";     // stored in String Pool (interned)
String s  = new String("Deepak"); // stored in Heap (not interned)


 ✅ String Pool & Interning in Java — Simple Explanation
 In Java, String is a special class, and Java optimizes memory for Strings using something called:
 ⭐ String Constant Pool (SCP)
 This is a special area inside the Heap memory where Java stores unique string objects.

 🟦 Case 1: String str = "Deepak";
 ✔ Stored in String Constant Pool (SCP)
 ✔ Java checks in SCP:
 "Is the string "Deepak" already present?"
 If YES → reuse the same object
 If NO → create a new object in SCP
 ✔ No new object is created if it already exists
 ✔ This string is interned (added to the pool)


 🟩 Meaning:
 All references with the same literal "Deepak" will point to the SAME object.
 Example:
 String a = "Deepak";
 String b = "Deepak";
 👉 Both a and b point to same memory object in SCP.

 🟧 Case 2: String s = new String("Deepak");
 ❌ NOT stored in String Pool
 ✔ Stored in Heap Memory
 ✔ A new object is created every time, even if "Deepak" is already in the pool.
 Example:
 String x = new String("Deepak");
 String y = new String("Deepak");
 👉 Here, two different objects are created in the heap.
 Even though both have the same value "Deepak", they are stored separately.

 🔥 Key Difference (VERY IMPORTANT)
 Code	                         Stored In	        Interned?	 Same ObjectReused?
 String s = "Deepak";	         String Pool(SCP)    YES	         YES
 String s =new String("Deepak");	Heap	          NO	          NO

 👉 Why does Java use String Pool?
 Because Strings are used a LOT (names, messages, file paths, etc.)
 If Java created new objects every time, memory would explode.
 So Java keeps only one copy of each unique literal.
 This is String Interning.

 ⭐ What is Interning?
 Interning means:
 Storing only one copy of a string in the String Pool and reusing it.

 You can manually intern a string too:
 String a = new String("Deepak");
 String b = a.intern();

 Now:
 b → points to String Pool object "Deepak"
 a → still points to heap object

 ✔ Final Example to Understand Clearly
 String p = "Deepak";
 String q = "Deepak";
 String r = new String("Deepak");


 Memory:
 SCP:
 "Deepak" ← used by p and q
 Heap:
 New "Deepak" object (for r)

 🔥 Comparisons
 p == q   // true (same object)
 p == r   // false (different objects)
 p.equals(r) // true (same content)





✅ 1.2   Immutability of Strings
⭐ Definition

A String in Java cannot be changed once created.
Any modification results in a new String object.

Example
String s = "Hello";
s.charAt(0) = 'Y';   // ❌ Not allowed — Strings are immutable
s = "Yellow";        // ✔ Allowed — new String object created

⭐ Why are Strings Immutable? (Very Important for Interviews)
✅ 1. Security

| Area                  | Why String Immutability is Important    |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| File paths            | Prevents unauthorized file access       |
| Class loading         | Ensures JVM loads correct classes       |
| Network URLs          | Prevents redirection to malicious sites |
| Database URLs         | Protects DB credentials and connections |
| Environment variables | Prevents system manipulation            |


Example
String password = "12345";
// If mutable → someone could modify password in memory 😱
→ Huge security risk!
Immutability protects against this.


✅ 2. String Pool Optimization (Memory Saving)
Java stores common strings in String Constant Pool (SCP).
String s = "Hello";
String x = "Hello";
Both refer to the same object.
📌 If Strings were mutable:
If x modified the shared object → s would also change
Entire JVM behaviour becomes unpredictable
Example (if mutable):
x.charAt(0) = 'M';
Then both become "Mello" → ❌ WRONG!

Immutability makes sharing safe.



✅ 3. Thread-Safety (No Locks Required)
Since a String cannot change:
multiple threads can use the same string safely
no need for synchronization
faster performance
fewer bugs

✅ 4. Better Hashing Performance (HashMap, HashSet)
HashMap stores keys based on:
hashCode()
equals()
Since String never changes:
hashCode remains constant
so HashMap lookup remains accurate

❌ If String were mutable:
Example:
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put("abc", 100);
// Suppose "abc" is modified to "xyz" in memory

What happens?
HashMap stored "abc" under bucket hash for "abc"
After mutation, hashCode changes!
But it's still in the old bucket…
👉 You can never find the key again
👉 Retrieval fails
👉 Map becomes corrupted

This is why Strings MUST be immutable.



✅ 2. Taking Input in Java
next()
* Reads only one word (stops at space)
String s = sc.next();

Input: Hello Deepak
Output: Hello

nextLine()
Reads entire line including spaces
String s = sc.nextLine();

Input: Hello Deepak
Output: Hello Deepak




✅ 3. charAt() & length()
charAt(index) → gives character at index
length() → gives total characters
String str = "Deepak";
str.charAt(3); // p
str.length();  // 6


✅ 4. indexOf()
Finds first occurrence of a character or string.
String str = "Deepak";
str.indexOf('e');    // 1
str.indexOf("pak");  // 3
str.indexOf('z');    // -1 (not found)



✅ 5. compareTo()
compares two strings lexicographically and returns a signed integer based
on the difference of the first unequal character.

It belongs to the Comparable interface, implemented by the String class.

🔥 Syntax
int result = string1.compareTo(string2);

🔥 Return Values (Interpretation)
| Return Value       | Meaning                                 | Explanation                                                           |
| ------------------ | --------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        0           | Strings are equal                       | All characters match and lengths are same                             |
|     > 0 (positive) | `string1` is greater than `string2`     | First non-matching character in `string1` has a  higher Unicode value |
|     < 0 (negative) | `string1` is smaller than `string2`     | First non-matching character in `string1` has a  lower Unicode value  |


🔥 How It Works Internally
Compares characters from left to right.
If characters are equal → move to next character.
If characters differ:
Return string1.char - string2.char
If one string ends but characters match so far:
Longer string is considered greater.

🔥 Examples
1. Equal Strings
"abc".compareTo("abc");   // 0

✔ All characters match → result is 0
2. First String Greater
"bbc".compareTo("abc");  // +1
'b' (98) - 'a' (97) = +1
✔ "bbc" comes after "abc" in dictionary order.

3. First String Smaller
"abc".compareTo("zbc");  // -25
'a' (97) - 'z' (122) = -25
✔ "abc" comes before "zbc".

4. Strings of Different Lengths
    String s = "Deepak";
    String d = "Deep";
    System.out.println(d.compareTo(s));
    System.out.println(s.compareTo(d));
👉 "Deep" is shorter
👉 "Deepak" is longer

📌 Java rule:
If one string ends, but all compared characters match,
then shorter string is considered smaller.

d.length() - s.length() = 4 - 6 = -2
s.length() - d.length() = 6 - 4 = +2

When strings match until one ends → shorter string is smaller.
"Deep" is shorter → so d.compareTo(s) = negative
"Deepak" is longer → so s.compareTo(d) = positive


🔥Key Notes
Comparison is case-sensitive
'A' < 'a' because 65 < 97
Uses Unicode values, not ASCII technically, but logic is same.
Useful for:
Sorting strings
Custom ordering



✅ 6. contains(), startsWith(), endsWith()

🔥6.1 contains()
Checks if the string contains a given substring anywhere.
Syntax
str.contains("substring");

Notes
Case-sensitive
Returns true if the substring exists anywhere in the string
Returns false otherwise

Example
"Deepak".contains("epa");   // true
"Deepak".contains("PAK");   // false

🔥 6.2. startsWith()
Checks if the string begins with the given prefix.
Syntax
str.startsWith("prefix");

Notes
Case-sensitive
Matching always starts from index 0
Example
"Deepak".startsWith("De");   // true
"Deepak".startsWith("ee");   // false

🔥 6.3. endsWith()
Checks if the string ends with the given suffix.
Syntax
str.endsWith("suffix");
Notes
Case-sensitive

Compares characters at the end of the string
Correct Examples
"Deepak".endsWith("pak");   // true   (last 3 letters = p a k)
"Deepak".endsWith("ak");    // true   (last 2 letters = a k)
"Deepak".endsWith("eep");   // false



✅ 7. Case Conversions
s.toLowerCase();
s.toUpperCase();


❗ Why String is Slow (Poor Performance)?

Because every modification creates a new object.

String str = "";
for(int i=0; i<10; i++){
    str = str + i;   // every time new string created
}

Time Complexity → O(n²)
Memory → wasted


✅ 8. concat()
String s3 = s1.concat(s2);
Same as:
String s3 = s1 + s2;



✅ 9. substring(i, j) & substring(i)
| Syntax          | Meaning       |
| --------------- | ------------- |
| substring(i, j) | from i to j-1 |
| substring(i)    | from i to end |


String s = "abcd";
s.substring(0,3); // "abc"
s.substring(2);   // "cd"




