✅ 12. equals() vs ==
== --> this is a comparator operator this will work on int , char , double
       but this will not work in String

| Operator    | What it checks               |
| ----------- | ---------------------------- |
| `==`        | Checks address (reference)   |
| `.equals()` | Checks actual content        |


String s1 = "hello";
String s2 = "hello";
String s3 = new String("hello");

s1 == s2  → true   (same address)
s1 == s3  → false  (different object)
s1.equals(s3) → true (same content)


s1.equals(s3) --> first will check the address if address is same then it
                  will return true if address is not same then it will
                  compare character by character then if all character of
                  string is same then it will return true



✅ 13. Java StringBuilder Class

StringBuilder is a mutable (changeable) string class used to build or modify strings efficiently.

⭐ 1) Why StringBuilder?
🔹 Problem With String   Strings are immutable

Every modification (+=) creates a new String object
This causes poor performance in loops or repeated operations

Example:

String str = "abc";
str += "xyz";
❌ Wrong understanding:
It does NOT “add after abc in the same memory”.

✔ Correct:
"abc" → stays in String Pool
"abcxyz" → new object created in Heap
str now points to new object

So every change = new object → slow.

⭐ 2) StringBuilder: Mutable (Changeable)
StringBuilder modifies the same object without creating new ones.

⭐ 3) charAt() vs setCharAt()
✔ String has:
char ch = str.charAt(0);   // allowed (read only)

❌ Not allowed:
str.charAt(0) = 'D';       // NOT allowed (String immutable)


✔ StringBuilder allows modification:
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("hello");
sb.setCharAt(0, 'D');   // modifies in place

Result:
"hello" → "Dello"
Because StringBuilder is mutable.



| Feature        | String           | StringBuilder                   |
| -------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Mutable?       | ❌ No            | ✔ Yes                           |
| charAt()       | ✔ Yes            | ✔ Yes                           |
| setCharAt()    | ❌ No            | ✔ Yes                           |
| Performance    | Slow for updates | Fast                            |
| Stored in SCP? | Yes (literals)   | No                              |
| Best Used For  | Fixed text       | Loops, appending, modifications |



🔥 14. StringBuilder Important Functions

->  Declaration of StringBuilder
 StringBuilder str = new StringBuilder();
 () inside we can give the capacity of str but when we declare not  the
 capacity of string then default capacity will be 16.


✔ setCharAt(index, char)  and ✔ append()
 str.setCharAt(0,'m');
 System.out.println(str);

append(int) , append(str)
sb.append("world");
sb.append(10);
sb.append('c');


✔ insert(index, char/string)  and ✔ deleteCharAt(index)
       01234
str = "hello";  --> str.insert(0,m)  --> mhello
str.deleteCharAt(0) --> hello

✔ delete(start, end)  and ✔ reverse()
Removes chars from start → end-1
0123456
physics
sb.delete(2,4); //  2 to 3 delete
System.out.println(sb); // phics






✅ Internal Working of StringBuilder
StringBuilder works using a mutable (changeable) char array inside it.
It keeps an internal buffer like this:
char[] value;
int count;   // number of characters currently used


⭐ 1. When you create StringBuilder
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("hello");
Internal buffer looks like:
value = ['h','e','l','l','o', _, _, _, _]   // extra empty space
count = 5
✔ StringBuilder allocates extra capacity so it can grow without creating new objects every time.


⭐ 2. When you append
sb.append("world");
StringBuilder does not create a new object.
It simply fills the same char[]:
value = ['h','e','l','l','o','w','o','r','l','d', _, _, _]
count = 10
✔ No new String created
✔ Same buffer reused → FAST


⭐ 3. If buffer becomes full → capacity expands
StringBuilder uses this formula:
newCapacity = oldCapacity * 2 + 2
This avoids frequent resizing.

Example:
Old capacity = 16
New capacity = 16*2 + 2 = 34
So, StringBuilder grows automatically.


⭐ 4. setCharAt() modifies directly
sb.setCharAt(0, 'D');
Internally:
value[0] = 'D';
✔ Direct update
✔ No new object
✔ This is why StringBuilder is FAST
✔ Cannot do this with String (because String is immutable)

⭐ 5. toString() creates final String
String finalString = sb.toString();
Now Java creates a brand-new String using the current char array.
This returns an immutable string.














✅ Java StringBuffer Class (Complete Notes)
⭐ What is StringBuffer?
StringBuffer is a mutable string class like StringBuilder, but it is thread-safe.
This means multiple threads can safely use the same buffer.
⭐ WHY StringBuffer exists?
Because:
String is immutable ❌ (slow in modifications)
StringBuilder is mutable but not thread-safe ❌
StringBuffer is mutable and thread-safe ✔ (synchronized)


⭐ Key Features
✔ 1. Mutable
You can modify the content without creating new objects.
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("hello");
sb.append("world");  // modifies same object
✔ 2. Thread-Safe (Synchronized)
Every method in StringBuffer is synchronized.
Meaning:
Only one thread can modify it at a time
Safe to use in multithreaded applications

Because of this:
StringBuffer is slower than StringBuilder
✔ 3. Stores characters in a mutable char[] buffer
Internal structure is like:

char[] value;   // buffer
int count;      // number of characters used

✔ 4. Capacity grows automatically
Default capacity = 16
If needed, new capacity = oldCapacity * 2 + 2

⭐ Common Methods
append()
insert()
delete()
deleteCharAt()
replace()
reverse()
setCharAt()
length()
capacity()

⭐ Example Usage (Simple)
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Hello");
sb.append(" Java");
System.out.println(sb);  // Hello Java

Example:
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Deep");
  // 🔹 Create StringBuffer
        StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Deep");
        System.out.println("Original: " + sb);

        // ⭐ 1. append()
        sb.append("ak");
        System.out.println("append(): " + sb);  // Deepak

        // ⭐ 2. insert()
        sb.insert(0, "Mr ");
        System.out.println("insert(): " + sb);  // Mr Deepak

        // ⭐ 3. delete(start, end)
        sb.delete(0, 3);
        System.out.println("delete(): " + sb);  // Deepak

        // ⭐ 4. deleteCharAt(index)
        sb.deleteCharAt(0);
        System.out.println("deleteCharAt(): " + sb);  // eepak

        // ⭐ 5. replace(start, end, string)
        sb.replace(0, 2, "De");
        System.out.println("replace(): " + sb);  // Deepak

        // ⭐ 6. setCharAt(index, char)
        sb.setCharAt(0, 'K');
        System.out.println("setCharAt(): " + sb); // Keepak

        // ⭐ 7. reverse()
        sb.reverse();
        System.out.println("reverse(): " + sb);  // kapeeK

        // ⭐ 8. length()
        System.out.println("length(): " + sb.length());

        // ⭐ 9. capacity()
        System.out.println("capacity(): " + sb.capacity());






✅ 2. What is Thread-Safety?
If multiple threads use the ``same object`` at the ``same time``,
and the program still works correctly → it is thread-safe.
Meaning:
No data corruption
No race conditions
No inconsistent data


❌ Problem Without Thread Safety
Imagine two threads modifying the same string:
Thread A:
sb.append("Hello ");
Thread B:
sb.append("World");
If they run at the same time → data may mix like:
HeWollrldo
This is called race condition.


⭐ Thread Safety prevents this.
✅ 3. How to make a class thread-safe?
By using synchronization.
That means:
Only ONE thread can modify the object at one time.
Others must wait.
It’s like a toilet lock:
Thread A locks → uses → unlocks
Thread B waits until free


⭐ StringBuffer is synchronized → thread-safe
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("A");
Every method (append(), delete(), insert(), etc.) is synchronized.
So:
If two threads try to modify sb
Only one will enter
No corruption
Output is correct



❗ Why StringBuilder is faster?
Because StringBuilder does NOT lock methods.
✔ Fast
❌ Not safe in multithreading
❓ So why do we need this in String classes?
Because strings get modified a lot in programs.
If multiple threads change the same string at the same time:
Data can corrupt
Output becomes wrong
Application can crash
So we use:
StringBuilder → fast, but not thread-safe, use in normal apps
StringBuffer → safe for multi-threaded code















